+{
+  "slug": "hair-anatomy-complete-guide",
+  "title": "Inside a Single Hair: Complete Anatomy Guide",
+  "h1": "Inside a Single Hair: Complete Anatomy Guide",
+  "description": "Have you ever wondered what is actually inside a single hair ? Although a hair strand looks simple from the outside, it is a complex biological structure with multiple layers, a living…",
+  "category": "Integumentary · Hair",
+  "updated": "2026-11-04",
+  "readTime": "6 min read",
+  "relatedOrgan": "skin",
+  "heroImage": "skin.webp",
+  "heroAlt": "Educational anatomy illustration: Inside a Single Hair: Complete Anatomy Guide",
+  "heroCaption": "Digital educational illustration for Inside a Single Hair: Complete Anatomy Guide. This plate is a learning aid, not a clinical photograph.",
+  "takeaways": [
+    "The hair shaft is the portion of the hair that projects above the surface of the skin. It is made of dead, keratinized cells and consists of three concentric layers:",
+    "Below the skin surface, the hair grows from a tunnel-like structure called the hair follicle . This is where the living part of the hair resides.",
+    "Sebaceous gland (oil gland): Attached to the follicle, it secretes sebum to lubricate the hair and skin, keeping them flexible and waterproof."
+  ],
+  "sections": [
+    {
+      "id": "1-the-hair-shaft-visible-part",
+      "heading": "1. The Hair Shaft (Visible Part)",
+      "paragraphs": [
+        "The hair shaft is the portion of the hair that projects above the surface of the skin. It is made of dead, keratinized cells and consists of three concentric layers:"
+      ],
+      "bullets": [
+        "Cuticle: The outermost thin layer made of overlapping, scale-like cells. It protects the inner layers and controls moisture movement.",
+        "Cortex: The middle and thickest layer. It contains bundles of keratin proteins and melanin pigment, which determine hair strength, texture, and color.",
+        "Medulla: The innermost core. It may be absent in fine hairs and is more prominent in thick, coarse hairs."
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "2-the-hair-follicle-the-living-anchor",
+      "heading": "2. The Hair Follicle (The Living Anchor)",
+      "paragraphs": [
+        "Below the skin surface, the hair grows from a tunnel-like structure called the hair follicle . This is where the living part of the hair resides.",
+        "Simple cross-section of a hair: Cuticle → Cortex → Medulla (center) Surrounded by: Hair follicle → Hair bulb → Dermal papilla"
+      ],
+      "bullets": [
+        "Hair root: The portion of the hair located within the follicle, beneath the skin surface.",
+        "Hair bulb: The expanded base of the follicle where the hair matrix cells divide rapidly to produce new hair.",
+        "Dermal papilla: A small nipple of connective tissue at the base of the bulb. It contains blood vessels that deliver oxygen and nutrients to the growing hair."
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "3-supporting-structures",
+      "heading": "3. Supporting Structures",
+      "bullets": [
+        "Sebaceous gland (oil gland): Attached to the follicle, it secretes sebum to lubricate the hair and skin, keeping them flexible and waterproof.",
+        "Arrector pili muscle: A tiny smooth muscle attached to the follicle. When it contracts (for example, from cold or fear), it pulls the hair upright — producing \"goosebumps.\""
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "4-what-gives-hair-its-strength-and-color",
+      "heading": "4. What Gives Hair Its Strength and Color?",
+      "bullets": [
+        "Keratin: A tough, fibrous structural protein that makes up the bulk of the hair shaft, giving it strength and resilience.",
+        "Melanin: The pigment produced by melanocytes in the hair bulb. Eumelanin creates brown/black tones; pheomelanin creates red/yellow tones. As we age, melanin production decreases, leading to gray or white hair."
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "5-hair-growth-cycle",
+      "heading": "5. Hair Growth Cycle",
+      "bullets": [
+        "Anagen (growth phase): Cells in the hair bulb divide actively; hair grows ~1 cm per month. Lasts 2–6 years.",
+        "Catagen (transition phase): Growth stops; the follicle shrinks. Lasts ~2–3 weeks.",
+        "Telogen (resting phase): The old hair is shed and a new cycle begins. Lasts ~3 months."
+      ]
+    }
+  ],
+  "questions": [
+    {
+      "question": "Q1. What is inside a single hair shaft?",
+      "answer": "The hair shaft has three layers: cuticle (outer), cortex (middle, with keratin and melanin), and medulla (inner core)."
+    },
+    {
+      "question": "Q2. What protein is hair mainly made of?",
+      "answer": "Hair is mainly made of keratin, a tough fibrous structural protein."
+    },
+    {
+      "question": "Q3. What gives hair its color?",
+      "answer": "Melanin pigment produced by melanocytes in the hair bulb determines hair color."
+    },
+    {
+      "question": "Q4. What is the dermal papilla?",
+      "answer": "The dermal papilla is a connective tissue nipple at the base of the hair bulb that supplies blood and nutrients to growing hair."
+    },
+    {
+      "question": "Q5. What causes goosebumps?",
+      "answer": "Contraction of the arrector pili muscles pulls hairs upright, producing goosebumps."
+    }
+  ],
+  "references": [
+    "Ross, M. H., & Pawlina, W. Histology: A Text and Atlas. Wolters Kluwer — chapters on integumentary system and skin appendages.",
+    "Marieb, E. N., & Hoehn, K. Human Anatomy & Physiology. Pearson — skin and hair follicles.",
+    "Corpus Anatomy Atlas — Integumentary System chapter (corpus-anatomy-atlas.netlify.app)."
+  ],
+  "seoTitle": "Inside a Single Hair: Anatomy and Growth | Corpus Atlas",
+  "seoDescription": "Explore hair anatomy: key structures, relationships, and function. Read five FAQs and cited references. For anatomy learning and review. For classroom use."
+}
